  "question": {
    "text": "The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is given by <math><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>, where <math><mi>L</mi></math> is the length of the pendulum and <math><mi>g</mi></math> is the acceleration due to gravity. The length is measured using a meter scale which has <math><mn>500</mn></math> divisions. If the measured value <math><mi>L</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math>, the accuracy in the determination of <math><mi>g</mi></math> is <math><mn>1.1</mn><mi>%</mi></math> and the time taken for <math><mn>100</mn></math> oscillations is <math><mn>100</mn></math> seconds, what should be the possible error in measurement of the clock in one minute (in milliseconds)?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "a5ork",
      "text": "<math><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
